t1 Compilation: Globals

t2 ZAEs Organization in compiler

t3 Scope parsing logic

p The global scope is the main scope and the \uhighest\u in our program; it \
has no parent. User code \uis\u in that global scope by default. Therefore, \
our unparsed ZCI list we have as entry point is defining instructions in \
\uglobal scope\u.

p The only way to create local scopes is to create \ufunctions\u, using \
\mDCL_FCT\m ZCIs which must be declared, in all cases, in \uglobal scope\u. \
Therefore, we must parse global scope ZCIs \bbefore\b local ones.

p By the way, because functions are declared as global ZCIs, their internal \
scope naturally has the global one as parent, and so, they are able to access \
to global ZAEs.

p This same logic applies for scopes declared inside functions (using \
statements for example): Local scopes always have access to their parent's \
content (upper scopes). As a result, we must parse \uhigher scopes\u first, \
and then finish with the lowest ones.


t3 Storing ZAEs

p Using that scope parsing logic, everything in a Z code can be stored using, \
for example, a tree like this:
"
CPL_instance
└── gbl_scp
    │
    ├── Type     tG1
    ├── Type     tG2
    ├── DataItem dG3
    ├── DataItem dG4
    │
    ├── Function A
    │   └── lcl_scp A
    │       ├── DatItm dA1
    │       ├── Exe    eA2
    │       └── Exe    eA3
    │
    ├── Function B
    │   └── lcl_scp B
    │       ├── DatItm    dB1
    │       ├── Statement eB2
    │       │   └── lcl_scp eB2
    │       │       ├── Exe eB2.e1
    │       │       └── Exe eB2.e2
    │       ├── Exe eB3
    │       └── Exe eB4
    │
    └── Function C
        └── lcl_scp C
            ├── DatItm dC1
            └── Exe    eC2
"

p There is a big difference between the global scope and local ones. The \
global scope is aim to be a \udefinition place\u to declare \ustatic\u things \
(types, functions, process-static memory items...). It \ucan't have dynamic\u \
things like local scope executions or any runtime dependant resources \
(function calls, statements...).

p Because of this, in ZINC, all the things that are global scope specific are \
not stored in it, but directly in the main compiler instance. Scope objects \
are mainly used for local purpose.

p They contain data items (static memory usage for the lifetime of the scope) \
and their executions (none for the global one) only. Of course, they still \
have access to their parent scope to use their declared data items. The tree \
structure looks more like this:
"
CPL_instance
├── types
│   ├── Type tG1
│   └── Type tG2
│
├── gbl_scp
│   ├── DataItems
│   │   ├── dG3
│   │   └── dG4
│   └── Exes
│
└── functions
    ├── Function A
    │   └── lcl_scp A
    │       ├── DataItems
    │       │   └── dA1
    │       └── Exes
    │           ├── eA2
    │           └── eA3
    │
    ├── Function B
    │   └── lcl_scp B
    │       ├── DataItems
    │       │   └── dB1
    │       └── Exes
    │           ├── eB2
    │           │   └── lcl_scp eB2
    │           │       ├── DataItems
    │           │       └── Exes
    │           │           ├── eB2.e1
    │           │           └── eB2.e1
    │           ├── eB3
    │           └── eB4
    │
    └── Function C
        └── lcl_scp C
            ├── DataItems
            │   └── dC1
            └── Exes
                └── eC2
"

p This way, scope related ZAEs can rely on generic elements such as types and \
functions, non-scope ZAEs. Therefore, it seems logic that we should parse them \
first, skipping every other ZCI for the moment. However, there is still one \
kind of ZAE to take into account before starting global parsing: Z modules.



t2 Step 1: Unmodulize

t3 Objective

p Z modules are an abstract representation of code that can contain both \
global and local ZAEs (functions declarations). However, every ZCI defined in \
a module is contained in a braces includer, which (as a recall) stays unparsed \
as raw text in the module declaration ZCI.

p As a consequence, to know whether a global ZCI is contained in a module, we \
must parse them first. This is why the first compilation step is called \
\bunmodulize\b. A module is nothing more than a couple of ZCIs where every \
declaration is to be preceeded by the given module prefix.

p The objective here is to replace every module declaration ZCI \mDCL_MOD\m in \
our ZCI list by all the ZCIs we could extract from the module. Also, these \
extracted ZCIs must keep the information of the module they were taken from, \
for further parsing steps.

p Also, Z modules can contain other sub-modules in themselves. Thus, we must \
also check the resulted extracted ZCIs to decompose every module completely. \
As a result, because modules must be declared in \uglobal scope\u, it should \
not have any remaining \mDCL_MOD\m ZCI in the code, and the current list of \
ZCIs to be parsed are all and only global scope related.


t3 Unmodulizing

p First we will look for module declaration ZCI using the \mmod\m keyword. It \
can be a module declaration (new module) or a module addition (module must \
already exist). Then, taking all the text defined between the module braces
includer, we extract global ZCIs from it using the same mechanism as in \
precompiler step P3.

p To keep track of module prefixing throughout submodule declarations, ZCI \
objects contains in themselves a module prefix text, empty by default. When \
module ZCIs are extracted, the resulted ZCIs are initialized with the module \
name \badded at the end\b of their \umodule prefix\u (modPfx \m""\m => \
\m"^MyModule."\m).

p Then, the current ZCI for module declaration/addition is removed and the \
extracted ZCIs are inserted here instead. Then, we can continue the \
unmodulization process on the newly extracted ZCIs.

p Naturally, if we have submodule declarations, the module prefix of inner \
ZCIs will be completed accordingly to the Z modularization logic: modPfx \
\m""\m => \m"^A."\m => \m"^A.^SubA."\m => \m"^A.^SubA.^SubSubA."\m => ...



